---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro'
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro'
import { url } from '../../lib/site'

/**
 * Traducción de cortesía de `/privacidad`, párrafo a párrafo y sin resumir: la
 * política española es la vinculante y el registro del art. 30 sigue viviendo
 * en el repo de la app (docs/legal/registro-actividades.md). Si cambia la
 * política en español, cambia esta traducción en el mismo commit.
 */
const actualizado = '24 September 2026'
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy — echo, dictation for Mac"
  description="Privacy policy of echo, the dictation app for Mac by Make Algo SL."
  path="/privacy"
  lang="en"
  translations={{ es: '/privacidad', en: '/privacy' }}
  ogImage="/og.png"
  ogImageAlt="echo, dictation for Mac that writes it clean"
>
  <Header lang="en" />
  <main id="contenido" class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose">
        <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-h2)">Privacy policy</h1>
        <p class="microcopy">
          This is a courtesy translation. The <a href={url('/privacidad')}>Spanish version</a> is
          the binding one; if the two differ, the Spanish text prevails.
        </p>

        <h2>Who is the data controller</h2>
        <p>
          Make Algo SL, Tax ID (NIF) B88875018, Calle General Pardiñas, 114 — 28006 Madrid, Spain.
          Email: <a href="mailto:hola@make-algo.com">hola@make-algo.com</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are three moments when we process your data, and they are different: when you write
          to us, when you subscribe and when you use echo on your Mac. They are set out separately
          because they have nothing in common. Apart from those is what only happens if you
          authorise it: the app's usage metrics.
        </p>

        <h2>If you write to us</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>What we collect.</strong> Your email address and whatever you tell us in the
          message. The email opens with the subject already filled in and two optional questions in
          the body —what you use echo for and whether you pay for Claude or ChatGPT—, but you decide
          what you answer and what you add. This website has no form and no database behind it: the
          message arrives in our mailbox and stays there.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What for.</strong> To read and answer your message, and to use what you tell us to
          understand how echo is used and improve it. Nothing else: no mailing list, we won't write
          to you afterwards, no newsletter, no advertising, no sharing your data with third parties
          and no automated decisions with legal effects on you.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Legal basis.</strong> Your consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR), which you give by the very
          act of writing to us. You can withdraw it at any time by writing to
          <a href="mailto:hola@make-algo.com">hola@make-algo.com</a>; withdrawing it does not affect
          the processing carried out until then.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>How long we keep it.</strong> Just long enough to reply and, at most, 12 months
          from your last message. If you ask us to delete it sooner, we delete it.
        </p>

        <h2>If you subscribe to echo</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>What we collect.</strong> Your email address, the licence key we issue to you, an
          identifier of each Mac where you activate it, the activation dates and the record that
          you accepted the terms when purchasing. <strong>We never see or store your card
          details</strong>: Stripe processes the payment from start to finish.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Mac identifier is a cryptographic digest of its internal serial number: it doesn't
          contain your name or say who you are, but it is tied to your licence, so we treat it as
          personal data and not as anonymous data.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What for.</strong> To issue your licence, so that echo can check it is still
          valid, to limit each key to the number of Macs you subscribed for, to help you if there is
          a problem, and to meet our accounting and tax obligations.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Legal basis.</strong> The performance of our contract with you (Art. 6(1)(b)
          GDPR). The record of your acceptance of the terms and the invoicing also rest on
          compliance with legal obligations (Art. 6(1)(c) GDPR).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>How long we keep it.</strong> For as long as you have the subscription and,
          afterwards, for the periods required by tax and commercial law, which run up to six years.
          That is why, if you ask for erasure during that period, what we do is
          <strong>block your data</strong> —it is taken out of use and kept available only for the
          tax authorities or a court— and erase it when the period ends. This is what Article 32 of
          the Spanish Data Protection Act (LOPDGDD) allows.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>If you only try echo and don't buy.</strong> We keep only that Mac's identifier
          and the date the trial started, so that the 14 days can't be restarted by reinstalling.
          Basis: our legitimate interest in the free trial working as advertised (Art. 6(1)(f)
          GDPR); the data doesn't identify you on its own and isn't used for anything else.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>If you use the referral programme.</strong> When someone subscribes with your
          code, we store that relationship (their subscription, your key and the code) to apply
          their discount, credit your balance and check there is no cheating (for example, inviting
          yourself with another email address or the same card). Of your invitees you will only see
          figures: how many people have used your code and how many rewards you have earned. We will
          never show you their email, their name or any other data, nor show them anything of yours.
          If you save an invitation code during the free trial, we remember it together with your
          Mac's identifier so we can apply the discount if you subscribe.
        </p>

        <h2>When you use echo on your Mac</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Your voice never leaves your computer.</strong> Speech recognition runs entirely
          on your Mac, offline.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>If you turn on AI polishing, the transcribed text does leave</strong>, but not
          towards us: echo runs on your Mac the official Anthropic or OpenAI application, which
          sends that text <strong>with your own account</strong>, just as if you had pasted it into
          their chat yourself. We don't see it, receive it or store it. That transfer is governed by
          the privacy policy of <a
            href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy"
            rel="noopener">Anthropic</a
          > or <a href="https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy" rel="noopener">OpenAI</a>,
          depending on which you use. If you'd rather nothing leaves, turn polishing off: echo keeps
          working.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>echo checks for new versions.</strong> To do so it requests, every few hours, a
          file hosted on this same website, which gives our hosting provider your IP address and the
          version you have installed. That information doesn't reach us, and you can turn off the
          automatic check in Settings.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Recordings stay on your Mac.</strong> echo can save the audio of your dictations
          so that you can compare recognition engines, but it <strong>comes turned off</strong> and,
          if you turn it on, those files don't leave your computer. The same goes for the activity
          log that echo writes in your logs folder.
        </p>

        <h2>App usage metrics (only if you say yes)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>We ask you when you set up echo, with two buttons and nothing pre-selected.</strong>
          If you say no, or don't answer, no identifier is created and nothing is sent: you don't
          appear in any record of ours. You can change your mind at any time in Settings › Usage
          metrics.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we count if you accept.</strong> Which setup steps you have completed and
          where you stopped, whether you reached your first dictation, and <strong>how many</strong>
          dictations, polishes and errors you make per day. Also the echo version, the macOS
          version, which recognition engine you use and which account you polish with. It is sent
          once at launch and once an hour, in a batch.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we never count.</strong> What you dictate, the audio, which app you paste
          the text into, your window titles and your IP address. The list of what can be sent is
          closed both in the app and on our server, and anything not on it is discarded on arrival.
          The rule we follow to decide whether something can be measured is: would it allow
          reconstructing what someone dictated or where they pasted it? If the answer is yes, it
          isn't measured.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The identifier is pseudonymous, not anonymous.</strong> It's a random number
          created when you accept; it doesn't derive from your machine or its serial number, and it
          is <strong>never linked</strong> to your licence's Mac identifier or to your email. But it
          stays with that installation over time —it's what lets us see whether people still use
          echo weeks later—, so we treat it as personal data and don't sell it to you as anonymous.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Legal basis.</strong> Your consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR).
          <strong>Withdrawing it isn't just stopping the sending:</strong> when you turn it off,
          echo asks our server to delete what that installation had sent, and it is deleted. What
          remains are counts with no identifier in them, because by then they are nobody's data.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>How long we keep it.</strong> For as long as the product exists or until you
          withdraw consent, whichever comes first.
        </p>

        <h2>Who else processes your data</h2>
        <p>We work with four providers, each for one thing:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>GitHub, Inc.</strong> (United States) hosts this website and the update feed.
            Like any hosting, it processes the technical data of your visit —for instance, the IP
            address— to serve the pages and protect them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Google</strong> (United States) provides the email service our mailbox runs on.
            Your message is sent by your own email program, with your provider.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cloudflare, Inc.</strong> (United States) provides the licence server. The
            database is hosted in its European region.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Stripe</strong> processes payments and handles your card details, which never
            pass through our systems.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          All four are certified under the <strong>EU–US Data Privacy Framework</strong>, the
          European Commission's adequacy decision that covers those transfers (Art. 45 GDPR). Beyond
          this, we don't share your data with anyone unless legally required.
        </p>

        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          You can ask us for access, rectification, erasure, objection, restriction of processing
          and portability by writing to <a href="mailto:hola@make-algo.com">hola@make-algo.com</a>.
          We will reply within one month at most. If you are a customer, bear in mind the blocking
          for tax retention periods explained above. If you believe we haven't handled it properly,
          you can lodge a complaint with the Spanish Data Protection Agency (<a
            href="https://www.aepd.es"
            rel="noopener">www.aepd.es</a
          >).
        </p>

        <h2>Cookies</h2>
        <p>
          This website uses no cookies, neither its own nor third parties'. If that ever changes, we
          will update this policy and, where the law requires it, ask for your consent first.
        </p>

        <h2>How we count visits</h2>
        <p>
          We want to know how many people visit this website and how many download echo, so when
          you open a page your browser tells us <strong>which page you opened, which website you
          came from and from which country</strong>. With that we add one to a counter, and that's
          it: we don't store your IP address, which browser you use, or any identifier that
          distinguishes you from someone else, so <strong>we can't reconstruct your visit or
          recognise you if you come back</strong>. Nor do we give it to anyone: the counter is on
          our own server and this website loads no third-party scripts —no Google Analytics or
          anything like it—.
        </p>
        <p>
          We do this on the basis of legitimate interest (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR): knowing whether the
          website is any use, which is the minimum needed to improve it. As it is an aggregate
          count, that interest doesn't compete with any right of yours, because no data that is
          yours remains. The counters are kept for as long as the product exists.
        </p>

        <p>
          The terms of service are in the
          <a href={url('/terms', 'en')}>terms of use and subscription</a>.
        </p>

        <p><em>Last updated: {actualizado}.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer lang="en" otroIdiomaHref={url('/privacidad')} />
</Base>
